WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.869
Hello, this is John from Technical Support.

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:07.941
We see some unusual activity on your computer
and we think you may be infected with the virus.

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:11.511
But don't worry, we have special disinfection software.

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:15.482
Go to this website, download that,
and it will help you clean your system.

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:18.451
Only problem with that scenario

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:20.920
is I'm not from technical support.

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:23.056
There's no virus on your computer.

00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:25.291
In fact, my name is not even John.

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:27.460
And in fact, that special disinfection

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.263
software -- that's about to make things worse.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.332
Let's take a look.

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:35.168
Okay, what just happened there?

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:37.971
Well, you've been hit by a RAT and a bad one.

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:40.073
And yes, there are, in fact, good ones.

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:42.208
But we're going to talk about the bad ones in this video.

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:46.846
What's a RAT? Well, it's a Remote Access Trojan.

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:49.783
A trojan is short for Trojan horse.

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:54.521
It's basically a piece of malware
that's wrapped inside another piece of software.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:59.359
So maybe it's a program that proclaims to do something good
or harmless or entertaining.

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:01.995
But in fact, there's bad stuff inside it.

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:05.398
A remote access refers to the fact

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:09.269
that a bad guy
is going to be able to remotely control your system.

00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:13.206
So here's our bad guy
and what he's going to be able to do once he has

00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:16.576
the RAT installed on your system, that special software,

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:22.282
he's going to be able to see everything
you type on your keyboard, including all your passwords.

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:27.120
In fact, he can even type things in as
if you had typed them in--complete control.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.290
He's going to see everything that comes up on your monitor.

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:35.161
So all the sensitive, confidential information
you might be reviewing--not only today,

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:38.898
but a week from now,
you'll still be able to see everything that you see.

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:42.368
You'll be able to have direct access to your disk.

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:47.807
You'll be able to put malware on there, maybe ransomware,
maybe encrypt all your files

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:52.412
and make you pay him in order to get that information
back--or just delete it all.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.982
He's going to have access to your webcam

00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:00.153
and to the microphone on your laptop.

00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:02.655
How about that for creepy?

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:05.725
In other words, he can turn these things on
even when you're not aware of it

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:08.962
and see everything you do and listen to everything you say.

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.764
That's what a remote access trojan would do.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:16.903
There's other things called remote access tools
that can be used by Help Desk for legitimate reasons

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:20.006
in order to do a couple of these kinds of things,
to see what's

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:23.376
working on your system and help you diagnose the problem.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:26.212
But in the hands of a wrong actor,

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.549
this becomes a RAT, a trojan.

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:32.986
And this guy has complete control over your system.

00:02:33.887 --> 00:02:37.223
Okay, let's see how these different types of infections

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:40.260
can occur where you become infected with a RAT.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:42.762
So the first one we already took a look at.

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:45.498
This is basically a social engineering attack.

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:49.202
And in this case, the bad guy is going to call this guy.

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:53.907
And when he does, he's going to instill in him
a sense of fear,

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:56.409
uncertainty and doubt. FUD.

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:58.344
There's going to be a sense of urgency.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:02.148
You need to do something now because we don't
want you to stop and really think about this.

00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:03.816
We want you to just act.

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:07.053
And what this guy is going to do is go on to his laptop.

00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:10.957
He's going to go over to the site
that is controlled by the bad guy.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:16.095
He's going to download this stuff
and now his system is infected with the RAT.

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:19.265
And this guy has complete control over his system.

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:21.601
Let's take a look at another example.

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:24.103
How about a pop up occurs?

00:03:24.571 --> 00:03:28.241
This guy is on his laptop, minding his own business,
surfing the web,

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:32.078
and all of a sudden a pop up appears on his system.

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:34.547
And the pop up gives him instructions.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:39.352
The instructions could be
that he just goes to this site and downloads

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:43.856
the disinfection software because it's telling him
“you've got a virus.” And by the way,

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:50.063
this is from some well-known antivirus company
that it claims to be that it really isn't.

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:54.500
Or it could be that it tells him to use his phone and call.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:58.671
And then the person he calls leads him
through the same type of scenario,

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:01.741
but the same results occur in both of these.

00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:04.744
The pop up is just the way to initiate the action.

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:09.449
But the user is actually infecting themself
in both of these cases.

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:15.054
And then one more that we'll take a look
at is a more silent kind of case.

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:17.357
In this case,

00:04:17.357 --> 00:04:21.594
they're going to get an email--it’s
going to come from the bad guy into his system.

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:26.099
And this email is like a phishing email
or something of that sort.

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:28.701
And it's going to tell him to click on a link.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:33.439
It may not tell him that he's been infected,
or it might, it could be either one of these circumstances.

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:38.911
It could say that it's coming from his bank and he needs to
install new software in order to access his account.

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:42.081
Or it could say we're from such and such

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:45.318
antivirus company
or such and such operating system company,

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:49.088
and we found the virus
and we need you to install the software.

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:51.190
Once again, same scenario.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.294
He goes over to the bad guy’s site, downloads

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:58.097
an app, downloads software,
and now he's under complete control.

00:04:58.298 --> 00:05:04.370
This guy now has a command and control server that allows
him to do whatever he wants on the good guy’s system.

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:09.879
Okay, so what can you do to prevent this from happening to you?

00:05:10.076 --> 00:05:12.278
Well, there's a lot of things. In fact,
that's the good news.

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:15.014
First and most important, have a good backup.

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:19.519
If everything else fails,
you want to be able to go to your backup and recover.

00:05:19.786 --> 00:05:22.121
So make sure you have that. Do that now.

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:25.591
Next thing is, make sure that you patch your systems.

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:29.796
I know it's inconvenient
sometimes to stop what you're doing and put on all of those

00:05:29.796 --> 00:05:33.066
vendor patches from all the different apps
and all the different OSes.

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:38.071
But if you don't, there may be a vulnerability
that the bad guy is going to exploit

00:05:38.271 --> 00:05:42.308
that gives him the remote access
trojan capability onto your system.

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:47.580
Another one is learn the tactics, techniques
and procedures (TTPs) of the bad guys.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.049
In other words, how do they go about doing what they do?

00:05:50.216 --> 00:05:53.953
If you understand how your adversary works,
then you'll have a much better

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:57.790
idea of how to avoid what they do
and you won't fall victim to it.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:58.958
So be aware.

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:00.660
Educate yourself.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.731
Then, if you do think
you need to call tech support for a problem.

00:06:04.764 --> 00:06:07.633
If they call you and report that they found an issue.

00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:10.703
I'm going to tell you, first of all, generally operating

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:14.107
system companies
do not call customers out of the clear blue.

00:06:14.307 --> 00:06:16.509
So you should suspect that something is up.

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:19.545
Neither do application vendors for the most part either.

00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:24.398
So if you do think for some reason
you want to check this out, say, 

00:06:24.398 --> 00:06:28.654
“Okay, I'll give you a call back,”
and don't take the number that they tell you.

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:34.394
Then you go and look up the number for that company
and you call them directly and then you can find out.

00:06:34.594 --> 00:06:39.165
So you do a separate call that you've initiated
and you know where you're actually calling.

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:43.069
Get all of your software from trusted sources.

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:46.372
Don't go to just any old app store and download stuff

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:51.310
because that may have a trojan inside it
and you may have just infected yourself.

00:06:51.644 --> 00:06:57.049
So as best possible,
know that your software comes from trusted sources.

00:06:58.017 --> 00:07:02.522
Use antivirus, endpoint detection and response software.

00:07:02.522 --> 00:07:06.492
The EDR stuff is used more in enterprise environments, AV,

00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:09.962
very common on user retail systems.

00:07:10.296 --> 00:07:13.833
But use these technologies
because they can sometimes detect

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:17.203
these RATs, the well-known ones,
and stop them in their tracks.

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:23.443
And then as another sort of safety measure,
use multi-factor authentication on all of your accounts.

00:07:23.443 --> 00:07:26.412
Don't let it just be a user
ID and password that gets you in

00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:29.582
because the guy controlling the RAT may--

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:34.020
remember --control your keyboard
and when you type in your password, they get your password.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:35.621
Then they can log in as you.

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:38.925
But if they also need to have possession of your phone

00:07:39.592 --> 00:07:44.030
in order to get your biometric to open the phone up
or to get into the app,

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:48.034
you know, with an SMS message that goes to your phone,
it makes it harder for them.

00:07:48.468 --> 00:07:51.270
Now, of course,
they could have a RAT on your phone as well.

00:07:51.437 --> 00:07:54.907
So there's a lot of different things
that could be involved here.

00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:59.245
But multi-factor authentication
certainly makes the job harder for the bad guy.

00:07:59.846 --> 00:08:07.353
And then one of the other things you could do
is basically turn off the camera or put a block, put a

00:08:07.353 --> 00:08:13.326
piece of tape, put something over the webcam on your laptop
and only take it off when you plan to use it.

00:08:13.693 --> 00:08:18.397
That way, if someone is on your system,
then they won't be able to see anything

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:21.434
and you'll have at least a little more peace of mind
in that case.

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:26.973
They actually make little devices that you can stick on
that allow you to slide and open and close the camera.

00:08:27.073 --> 00:08:29.342
So look for that kind of capability as well.

00:08:29.942 --> 00:08:31.944
So there's a lot of things you can do here.

00:08:31.978 --> 00:08:33.212
That's the good news.

00:08:33.212 --> 00:08:35.882
And I'll just say, if John from Tech Support

00:08:35.882 --> 00:08:38.851
gives you a call, best thing you could do, just hang up.

00:08:39.352 --> 00:08:40.720
Don't get infected by a RAT.

00:08:42.088 --> 00:08:43.189
Thanks for watching.

00:08:43.189 --> 00:08:46.058
If you like this video, please like and subscribe.

00:08:46.058 --> 00:08:51.130
And don't forget to hit the notify button so that you'll be
aware of other videos like this when they come out.

